#!/bin/sh
# Blocks a commit that has not passed the manifest's commit tier (~70s).
#
# Runs against the WORKING TREE, not the staged index: several checkers resolve paths relative
# to the repository root and one additionally scans an external skills checkout, so gating an
# exported index would silently change the corpus they scan. The exact gate is the push receipt,
# which is keyed to the tree actually being pushed. One consequence: "pre-commit passed" describes
# the working tree at commit time, not necessarily what a partial `git add` actually committed —
# a commit built from a subset of the working tree's changes is not itself re-verified here, only
# at push time, when the receipt's tree-hash check covers exactly what gets pushed.
#
# EXEMPTION, reasoned rather than a convenience carve-out — this is the one place in the project
# where the tier is deliberately skipped rather than run, and only while one of a rebase,
# cherry-pick, revert, or merge is GENUINELY unfinished: a queued step still in that operation's own
# todo file, or an unresolved conflict sitting in the index. A sequencer marker with neither of
# those — a clean `git merge --no-commit --no-ff` about to be concluded by an ordinary `git commit`,
# or a cherry-pick/revert whose conflict is already resolved and staged — is NOT exempted; that
# commit gates normally like any other. The narrower case still matters: while genuinely mid a
# multi-commit rebase/cherry-pick/revert, git can invoke this hook once per replayed commit,
# multiplying the ~70s tier by the commit count — with an agent's shell cap, that can exceed the
# available time with no permitted escape. None of the replayed commits is ever pushed in
# isolation: the sequencer produces ordinary commits inside one operation, and `pre-push`'s
# tree-keyed receipt still gates the eventual push and independently refuses on a dirty tree, so
# skipping here removes redundant intermediate work without letting anything unverified reach the
# remote. The skip requires the git dir's own filesystem markers to match the SHAPE of a real
# sequencer operation that ACTUALLY has work left, validated by `git-sequencer-state.mjs`: a
# `*_HEAD` file must contain an id that resolves to a real object in this repository, a rebase
# directory must carry the sibling files git itself writes there (one of which is checked the same
# way), and the remaining-work signal (a todo file's queued lines, `rebase-apply`'s `next`/`last`
# counters, or the index's own unmerged entries) is read from files git itself writes, never
# inferred. There is no flag, environment variable, or plain marker file that triggers this by
# itself. HONEST LIMIT: git's own sequencer state is filesystem-trust-based, so this cannot be
# absolute either — a deliberate, informed forgery (writing a real, resolvable object id and a
# plausible todo/counter file into the right paths) is not something any detector reading only the
# working tree can rule out. Nor is the skip rare under ordinary use: a real conflict left
# unresolved for a while reaches it with no forgery at all — an abandoned conflicted operation
# keeps skipping for as long as it sits there, which is why `git-sequencer-state.mjs` prints a loud,
# specific stderr message naming the state and the exact command to conclude it every time this
# fires, rather than the single quiet line below. What this guarantees is that the skip requires
# reproducing the SHAPE of a real, unfinished sequencer state, not that it is unforgeable or hard to
# reach by accident.
set -e

sequencer_state=$(node scripts/git-sequencer-state.mjs)
if [ -n "$sequencer_state" ]; then
    exit 0
fi

exec node scripts/run-gate-tier.mjs commit
